# Routes and Handlers

## Overview

Routes decide what an Agent does with each Signal. They form one ordered
table: Jido checks it top to bottom, takes the first route whose path
matches the Signal type, and runs that route's handler. One Signal, one
handler.

This Livebook builds an order Agent whose route table looks like this:

```text
order.create     -> Routes.Record          Action module
order.note       -> inline action          Action defined in the route
order.checkout   -> Routes.Checkout        Flow
order.ship       -> inline action          emits only "shipping.requested"
partner.order    -> transform              rewrites to "order.create"
audit.ping       -> pass-through           re-emits the Signal unchanged
order.*          -> Routes.Record          one-segment wildcard
**               -> Routes.Record          catch-all
```

You'll run a Signal through each kind of handler, then cover wildcards,
defaults, emission contracts, and the mistakes Jido rejects at compile time.

| Handler | Runs | Changes state |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Action | One `Jido.Action` module or inline `action` block | Yes |
| Flow | One `Jido.Flow` | Yes |
| Transform | A function from Signal to Signal | No |
| Pass-through | Nothing | No |

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## Supporting Modules

One Action records which handler ran. A Flow computes an order total in three
steps and finishes with an Action that writes it to state.

```elixir
defmodule Routes.Record do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "record",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{handler: Zoi.string()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{handler: handler}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | last: handler}}
  end
end

defmodule Routes.Total do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "total",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{subtotal: Zoi.integer(), tax: Zoi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{subtotal: subtotal, tax: tax}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | total: subtotal + tax}}
  end
end

defmodule Routes.Checkout do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "checkout",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{price: Zoi.integer(), quantity: Zoi.integer()})

  flow do
    step "subtotal", [price <- input(:price), quantity <- input(:quantity)] do
      {:ok, %{value: price * quantity}}
    end

    step "tax", subtotal <- result("subtotal", :value) do
      {:ok, %{value: div(subtotal, 10)}}
    end

    step "total",
      action: Routes.Total,
      params: %{subtotal: result("subtotal", :value), tax: result("tax", :value)}

    output result("total")
  end
end
```

A Flow on an Agent route must output the complete next Agent state.
`Routes.Total` reads `context.agent_state`, so its result is the whole state.

## The Route Table

```elixir
defmodule Routes.Orders do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "orders"

  agent do
    schema(
      Zoi.object(%{
        last: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.default(""),
        total: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0),
        notes: Zoi.list(Zoi.string()) |> Zoi.default([])
      })
    )
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/routes")

    route "order.create", Routes.Record, as: :create, defaults: %{handler: "create"}

    route "order.note", as: :note do
      action %{text: text},
        schema: Zoi.object(%{text: Zoi.string()}),
        context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | notes: [text | context.agent_state.notes]}}
      end
    end

    route "order.checkout", Routes.Checkout, as: :checkout

    route "order.ship", as: :ship, emits: ["shipping.requested"] do
      action _input, schema: Zoi.object(%{}), context: context do
        {:ok, context.agent_state,
         [emit("shipping.requested", %{last: context.agent_state.last})]}
      end
    end

    route "order.leak", as: :leak, emits: ["shipping.requested"] do
      action _input, schema: Zoi.object(%{}), context: context do
        {:ok, context.agent_state, [emit("billing.charged", %{})]}
      end
    end

    route "partner.order", as: :partner, emits: ["order.create"] do
      transform %Jido.Signal{data: %{ref: ref}} = signal when is_binary(ref) do
        {:ok, %{signal | type: "order.create", data: %{handler: "partner:" <> ref}}}
      end
    end

    route "audit.ping", as: :ping, emits: ["audit.ping"]

    route "order.*", Routes.Record, defaults: %{handler: "order wildcard"}
    route "**", Routes.Record, defaults: %{handler: "fallback"}
  end
end

agent = Routes.Orders.instantiate!(id: "orders-1")

Enum.map(Routes.Orders.definition().routes, &{&1.path, &1.handler.kind})
```

The result lists each route in declaration order:

```text
[{"order.create", :action}, {"order.note", :action}, {"order.checkout", :flow},
 {"order.ship", :action}, {"order.leak", :action}, {"partner.order", :transform},
 {"audit.ping", :pass_through}, {"order.*", :action}, {"**", :action}]
```

Inline `action` blocks compile to Action modules, so they report `:action`.

| Route part | Rule |
| --- | --- |
| path | An exact Signal type, or a pattern with `*` or `**`. Unique in the Agent. |
| handler | A module, one `action` or `transform` block, or nothing (pass-through). |
| `as:` | Generates `<name>_signal/2` and `<name>_signal!/2`. Exact paths only. |
| `defaults:` | Input values under the Signal data. Action and Flow routes only. |
| `emits:` | The Signal types the route may emit. Omit it to leave the route unchecked. |

## Action Modules

The Signal data becomes the Action's input.

```elixir
signal = Routes.Orders.create_signal!(%{handler: "mine"})
{:ok, candidate, []} = Routes.Orders.cmd(agent, signal)

candidate.state.last
```

The result is `"mine"`.

## Inline Actions

An inline block is a full Action with its own schema. The first argument is a
pattern over the validated input. `context:` binds the Turn context. `emit/1,2`
is imported in every Agent module.

```elixir
signal = Routes.Orders.note_signal!(%{text: "fragile"})
{:ok, candidate, []} = Routes.Orders.cmd(agent, signal)

candidate.state.notes
```

The result is `["fragile"]`. Move the block to a module when a second Agent
needs it or it grows past a few lines.

## Flows

A Flow route runs every step in one Turn and commits once.

```elixir
signal = Routes.Orders.checkout_signal!(%{price: 250, quantity: 4})
{:ok, candidate, []} = Routes.Orders.cmd(agent, signal)

candidate.state.total
```

The result is `1100`: a subtotal of 1,000 plus 100 tax. If any step fails,
the Turn fails and no state changes. The
[Jido Action Flow guide](https://hexdocs.pm/jido_action/flows.html) covers
the Flow language.

## Transforms

A transform translates an inbound Signal into one outbound Signal. It runs no
Action and leaves state unchanged. It returns `{:ok, signal}` or
`{:error, reason}`.

```elixir
signal = Routes.Orders.partner_signal!(%{ref: "A-17"})
{:ok, candidate, [emit]} = Routes.Orders.cmd(agent, signal)

{candidate == agent, emit.signal.type, emit.signal.data}
```

The result is `{true, "order.create", %{handler: "partner:A-17"}}`: no state
change and one `Emit` Directive.

`%{signal | ...}` keeps every other envelope field, including `id`. Build a
new Signal with `Jido.Signal.new/3` when the output needs its own `id`.

A Signal that doesn't match the transform's pattern fails the Turn:

```elixir
signal = Routes.Orders.partner_signal!(%{ref: 17})
{:error, error} = Routes.Orders.cmd(agent, signal)

{error.code, Exception.message(error)}
```

The result is `{:transform_input, "Signal does not match a transform clause"}`.
To accept several shapes, write `transform do ... end` with one `->` clause
per shape.

## Pass-Through

A route with no handler emits the incoming Signal unchanged.

```elixir
signal = Routes.Orders.ping_signal!(%{n: 1})
{:ok, candidate, [emit]} = Routes.Orders.cmd(agent, signal)

{candidate == agent, emit.signal.type, emit.signal.id == signal.id}
```

The result is `{true, "audit.ping", true}`. If a pass-through route declares
`emits:`, the list must include its own exact path. Parent Agents use
pass-through to forward Signals to members; see
[Connect Agent Outputs](connect-agent-outputs.livemd).

## Wildcards And Order

`*` matches exactly one segment. `**` matches any number of segments. The
first matching route wins, so put exact routes first and catch-alls last.

```elixir
for type <- ["order.cancel", "order.cancel.bulk", "user.login"] do
  signal = Jido.Signal.new!(type, %{}, source: "/guides/routes")
  {:ok, candidate, []} = Routes.Orders.cmd(agent, signal)
  {type, candidate.state.last}
end
```

The result is:

```text
[{"order.cancel", "order wildcard"},
 {"order.cancel.bulk", "fallback"},
 {"user.login", "fallback"}]
```

`order.cancel.bulk` has two segments after `order`, so `order.*` doesn't match
it. Without the `**` route, an unknown type fails its Turn with
`"No route accepts this Signal type"`. Leave the catch-all out unless you want
every unknown Signal handled.

## Defaults And Helpers

Route `defaults:` fill input the Signal data leaves out. Signal data wins on
equal keys. The merge is shallow: a nested map in the data replaces the
default map.

```elixir
signal = Routes.Orders.create_signal!()
{:ok, candidate, []} = Routes.Orders.cmd(agent, signal)

candidate.state.last
```

The result is `"create"`, from the route default.

`as: :create` generated `create_signal!(data \\ %{}, opts \\ [])`. It builds a
Signal and runs nothing. It raises when the data isn't a plain map or an
option is invalid. `create_signal/2` takes the same arguments and returns
`{:ok, signal}` or `{:error, reason}`. Neither validates the data against the
Action schema; the Turn does that. `opts` accepts `:source`, `:id`, `:time`, `:subject`,
and `:datacontenttype`. The default source is the Agent's `signal_source`.

## Emission Contracts

With `emits:`, Jido checks every `Emit`, `AgentSend`, and `OwnerSend` the
route returns against the list, on every Turn.

```elixir
ship = Routes.Orders.ship_signal!()
{:ok, _candidate, [emit]} = Routes.Orders.cmd(agent, ship)

leak = Routes.Orders.leak_signal!()
{:error, error} = Routes.Orders.cmd(agent, leak)

{emit.signal.type, error.code, Exception.message(error)}
```

The result is
`{"shipping.requested", :undeclared_emission, "Signal type is outside the route contract"}`.
The `leak` route emitted `billing.charged`, so its whole Turn failed.

## Compile-Time Errors

These fail the build with a `Spark.Error.DslError`:

- two routes with the same path;
- `as:` on a wildcard path, or one helper name used twice;
- `defaults:` on a transform or pass-through route;
- a pass-through `emits:` list without the route's own path.

A target module together with a `do` block, or a block with more than one
`action` or `transform`, fails with a `CompileError`.

